# AGENTS.md: conventions for anyone (or any agent) changing this repository

- `make check` is the gate, offline, under two minutes. If it is green and the pull request
  names what a user can do afterwards, it merges. Do not add stations to it.
- Records state publisher facts; tools state verification facts. Never write a level, a
  checksum or a "verified" word into a record.
- Schemas are contracts. A change to `schema/*.json` is a kernel change: bump the `$id`
  version, keep a test that the old files still validate, name the change in the PR title.
- The site is surface: one HTML, one JS, one CSS, no build step, no framework. Deletable modules,
  never migrations. A screenshot is its proof.
- Network only in `osd verify`, the `verify` workflow, the one page `osd new <url>` reads for a title (`--title` skips it), `osd get <manifest>`, which is intentional retrieval by the person running it, and `osd mirror put`, which copies eligible origins into the mirror (run by the verify workflow on main, or by a maintainer), and `osd mirror deposit` and `osd mirror put-derived`, which upload files already on the machine to the mirror store and reach nothing else (E41). Tests inject a fetcher or transport.
- Lanes claim paths in `docs/program/CLAIMS.md` before they start; two open lanes never share a
  path. Branch names are `e<nn>-<slug>`; one squash merge per lane.
- Program state lives in `docs/program/PROGRAM.md` (queue), `docs/program/charters/` (what each
  epic must achieve) and `docs/program/DECISIONS.md` (open questions for the owner).
- Keep the conversation small: read the lines you need, write results to files, brief by reference.
# Architecture

One sentence: contributors write facts about open datasets, tools turn those facts into verified
levels, outlines and a catalogue, and a static map plus a manifest let anyone find and fetch the
data; Ophiolite will read the same catalogue as a source (its E127; today a contract and a refusing stub).

## The chain

```
  SEG wiki, national        contributor            verifier            outline extractor
  registers, publishers ──► records/*.yaml ──────► build/verification ─► outlines/*.geojson
        (lists)             (facts, CC0)            (levels L1–L2)        (L3, derived)
                                 │                        │                    │
                                 └──────────┬─────────────┴────────────────────┘
                                            ▼
                                     osd build  ─────►  build/catalog/
                                                        records.json · outlines.geojson · collections.json (STAC)
                                            │
                 ┌──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┐
                 ▼                          ▼                              ▼
           site/ (MapLibre)           QGIS / any GIS                 manifest (E06)
           toggles, search,           GeoJSON / STAC by address      osd get · Python reader
           panel, cards                                                   │
                                                                          ▼
                                                              Ophiolite source (E09, L4)
                                                              E85 intake · E35 rights-basis
```

## Components and their boundaries

| Component | Owns | Never does |
|---|---|---|
| **Records** (`records/`, `schema/record.schema.json`) | Facts as the publisher states them: origin, licence, kinds, region | Carry a level, a checksum or anything a tool produced |
| **Verifier** (`src/osd/verify.py`, `schema/verification.schema.json`) | Network checks, checksums, licence text capture, the level ladder | Change a record; run inside the required CI check |
| **Outlines** (`outlines/`, `schema/outline.schema.json`, extractor in E03) | Geometry per wellbore or survey in WGS84, with its derivation named | Invent geometry without saying `approximate: true` |
| **Build** (`src/osd/build.py`) | Joins records, outlines and levels into the published files | Fetch anything |
| **Catalogue** (`build/catalog/`) | The one published artefact; STAC-shaped collections, GeoJSON outlines, records JSON | Hold dataset bytes |
| **Site** (`site/`) | Reading the catalogue and showing it; copyable addresses and snippets | Hold its own data store; need a server |
| **Retrieval** (E06) | A manifest from a selection; a fetcher that verifies checksums | Decode proprietary project files (Ophiolite connector doctrine) |
| **Mirror store** (E07) | Checksummed copies of small or orphaned datasets whose licence allows it | Copy anything with `redistribution: unknown` |
| **Ophiolite bridge** (E09) | The catalogue as an Ophiolite source; rights-basis from the record; fidelity records | Live outside the Ophiolite monorepo's own conventions |
| **Starters** (E40; `starters/`, `schema/starter.schema.json`, `src/osd/starters.py`) | One committed document per starter pack, written by its recipe; the nine validity tests; `starters.json` and the pack manifest | Hold artefact bytes (they live in the mirror under custody/2), publish a pack it could not check, or let anyone write a pack by hand |

## Formats, all standard

- Records: YAML validated by JSON Schema 2020-12. Outlines: GeoJSON, EPSG:4326.
- Catalogue: STAC 1.0 collections and items (full validity is E04), plus one GeoJSON for map and GIS.
- Tiles: PMTiles once outlines exceed what one GeoJSON serves comfortably (E05).
- Manifest: `osd.manifest/1` JSON (E06). Seismic store, if ever: Zarr-based (E10).

## The level ladder

L0 listed → L1 reachable → L2 checksummed and licence captured → L3 outlined from the data →
L4 converted to a canonical type with a fidelity record. No rung is skipped, and a level is
computed from check outcomes, never typed. The ladder is the product's honesty: a dashed outline
on the map is a placeholder and says so.

## What the map is and is not

The map is a view of the catalogue, nothing more. It has no account, no state beyond the URL, no
server. Everything it shows can be opened in QGIS by one address. That is also why the website
and "a GIS layer" are one deliverable rather than two.

## Where Ophiolite comes in

Ophiolite is a consumer of the catalogue, and the catalogue borrows Ophiolite's vocabulary
(kinds from the E55 registry, rights-basis from E35). The two repositories meet at one interface:
a manifest or STAC collection Ophiolite's intake (E85) can read. No Ophiolite code runs here;
no catalogue code runs there.
# Engineering setup

Fit for purpose: a Python package, YAML records and a static page. The Ophiolite program
(docs/program-2026-09-25 in the Ophiolite monorepo) taught what to keep and what to drop; the
evidence for each line is in the Ophiolite program records of 8–22 October 2026.

## Keep (copied from Ophiolite)

| Practice | Why it earned its place |
|---|---|
| Claims board: a lane names its paths before it starts, two lanes never share a path | Overlap was the cause of merge pain; the 21 Oct simplification cut kept the board deliberately |
| Contract first: slice 0 is schema, fixtures and stubs; later slices vertical | Layer-cut slices made serial chains (18 Oct rule) |
| Kernel vs surface proof bar | Surface strategies capped at one review round after revisions ran to 8 and 18 rounds on E60 and E42c |
| Outcome-named slices: the brief says what a user can do after merge, or no lane | Success metric is slices merged per day, not green CI |
| Revert first: a red main gets one revert within the hour, the fix follows | Owner accepted a briefly red main on about one merge in five |
| No artificial schedule: act when the precondition holds, never on a timer | Nightly timers and self-set dates cost days (night 2026.10.95) |
| Small fixes batched, one push per merge, no wait on the repeat run | Loop steps 7 and 8 |
| Brief by reference, one drafting and one execution agent, small contexts | 83 % of usage was sessions over 150k tokens, 78 % subagent-heavy |
| A different model reviews kernel charters, read-only, one round | Reviews caught contract mistakes; multiple rounds did not pay |

## Drop (what cost Ophiolite more than it returned)

| Station | Evidence | Here instead |
|---|---|---|
| Self-hosted runners, pool builders, runner crews | needrestart killed runner units after any apt run; two crews and "chair out, station out" existed only to manage them | GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-24.04`, one job, under two minutes |
| Merge trains, then a merge queue | 8 workers gave 10× activity for 2–3× merges; merges per worker-day fell from 5.6 to 0.3 at 18 workers | Squash merge on a green check; at most 2 coding lanes plus 1 records lane |
| Nightly releases, release pinning, qualification | A night took 1 h 50 min at best, ARM64 70 min on the critical path; per-lane qualification 45 min serial | Every merge to main publishes the site; no version numbers |
| Hardening lanes and a growing item list | 87 hardening merges against about 10 epic slices in a week; the list grew 150 → 217 | Defects under 50 lines ride on the next lane; a red main is reverted, not hardened |
| Committed conformance results, locks, worker-check cron | Three lanes lost 30–45 min each on a dead lock; a queue stopped for an hour on a docs-only deadline | Nothing is committed that a job can regenerate; no locks; no cron |
| Multiple Codex rounds, 4-revision caps | 2–4 h of a worker per round | One round for a kernel charter; none for surface or records |
| Dispatch per commit group | Worth it for a 27-minute C++ build | A 2-minute check runs on every push; nothing to batch |
| ccache, proven-tree skip, CI spend caps | Needed for a 27-minute C++ build | Irrelevant at Python-and-static scale; revisit above 10 min per check |

## The gate

`make check` = ruff + pytest + schema validation + build, offline. It is the only required
status. Network verification (`verify` workflow) comments on pull requests and never blocks: a
publisher's outage is a finding, not a red gate. The site publishes from main on every merge.

## Proof bar by class

- **Kernel** (schemas, verifier, build, manifest, bridge): tests for every acceptance line, a
  mutation shown to fail for every guard, one Codex round on the charter. Strategy at lane open,
  at most two pages.
- **Surface** (site, docs, records): `make check` green and a screenshot or the verify comment.
  No strategy document; the charter's acceptance list is the brief.
- **Records**: the verify comment shows the level; a human checks the licence fields against
  the publisher's page. Same-day merge.

## Roles

- **Supervisor** (one): keeps `docs/program/PROGRAM.md` true, writes lane briefs by reference,
  merges on green, reverts on red, records decisions in `DECISIONS.md`. Does not write product
  code except a revert.
- **Worker** (at most three concurrent: two coding lanes, one records lane): takes a charter,
  claims paths, opens a branch `e<nn>-<slug>`, pushes when the diff is inside the claim, opens
  the pull request with the outcome sentence and the proof. Waits for nothing but its own check.
- **Reviewer** (Codex, read-only): one round per kernel charter, dispositions recorded in the
  charter.

## What is deliberately absent

No merge queue, no release branches, no version tags, no nightly, no self-hosted compute, no
lock files committed by hand, no conformance ledger, no item list. If one of these starts to look
necessary, the measured reason goes into `DECISIONS.md` first.
# The map as a shop (E46–E53): one page where a person finds, picks and takes open subsurface data

Authority: owner, 25 Nov 2026. The design was drawn in rounds on Paper (file "OpenSubsurfaceData",
page "Where we want to be: the map as a shop", boards Shop 01–06; page "Open questions", boards
Open 01 and 02, both decided) and accepted with "go with your recommendations" / "go with all your
recommendations" / "go with your advice". The owner's rulings are OSD-D26 to OSD-D31. The working
prototype and the three studies are at `~/.local/share/osd-program/prototype-2026-11-25/` on the
controller (`index.html`, `build_areas.py`, `speed-practices.md`, `global-layers.md`,
`block-layers.md`; `b6.py` holds the text of every board). The prototype is evidence of the design,
not source: every lane rebuilds in this repository's structure and copies nothing in.

Builds on E05 (map, hash state, Playwright site test), E06 (select, manifest/4, `osd get`), E15 and
E36 (tiles, well grids, first load under 1 MB), E21 and E27 (when, why), E32 (build shape), E43
(starter cards), E07 (mirror).

**Review.** Kernel charters E46, E49 and E51 each get one Codex round (OSD-D8); dispositions are
recorded under each charter. E47, E48, E50, E53 (Surface) and E52 (Records) get none.

**Why this family exists.** Today the site is a map with a filter panel, a detail panel and three
side pages (How, Status, Starter projects). A person who arrives cannot see at a glance what exists
where they are looking, the map draws a rectangle for a dataset that covers a country, and taking
data means a manifest and a command line. The agreed design turns the one page into a shop: look at
a place, see the list for that place, add to a bag, leave with the files, a Python file, or (later)
an Ophiolite project.

